= Texas

== Officer and Director Liability

=== SB 29 writes the business judgment rule into the statute.

- Good afternoon, and welcome. Today we take up Texas, which took the opposite order of operations from Nevada by building the court first, then the corporate law, then the exchanges.

- I want to trace a single through-line today. Texas is selling a corporate-law product, and the interesting question is who the product is for and how much of it is actually deliverable. 

- We start with the substantive answer to Delaware. Senate Bill 29, which took effect immediately in May 2025, wrote the business judgment rule into the statute at Section 21.419.

- To get it, a corporation must have voting shares listed on a national securities exchange, or it must affirmatively elect into the section in its governing documents.

- I want to be extremely precise about who this covers. Subsection (b) describes the presumptions as concerning duties owed "to a domestic corporation." 

- This is domestic-only, meaning it sorts among Texas corporations and does not reach a Delaware company that simply dual-lists on a Texas exchange. For an unlisted Texas company, the opt-in lets the board take the whole package by adopting a bylaw.

- The presumptions are in subsection (c): a director or officer is presumed to act in good faith, on an informed basis, in furtherance of the interests of the corporation, and in obedience to the law and the governing documents.

- To state a claim, the plaintiff must rebut one or more of those, and prove a breach that involved fraud, intentional misconduct, an ultra vires act, or a knowing violation of law. And subsection (f) requires pleading those four with particularity. 

- Notice that the list is almost identical to Nevada's floor, except Texas adds the ultra vires act. The doctrinal distance between them is smaller than the marketing suggests.

=== SB 2411 exculpation reaches officers too.

- Liability is one thing; money damages are another. Section 21.419 determines whether there is liability, and Section 7.001 limits money damages.

- Senate Bill 2411, effective September 1, 2025, amended Section 7.001 to extend exculpation to officers to the same extent already permitted for directors. 

- It excludes breaches of the duty of loyalty, intentional misconduct, transactions involving an improper benefit, and statutory violations. 

- Note the difference in how you get there. Section 21.419 may be adopted in the governing documents, which includes the bylaws, so a board can take the whole package on its own. 

- Section 7.001 exculpation requires an affirmative election in the certificate of formation, which means you have to go to the shareholders and get a vote. 

=== A committee can be blessed before the transaction exists.

- Now we get to the machinery, which is perhaps the most aggressive piece of the package.

- Section 21.416(g) lets a board form a standing committee of independent and disinterested directors to approve transactions involving a controlling shareholder, director, or officer, whether or not the transaction is contemplated at the time the committee is formed.

- Then Section 21.4161 lets the corporation petition the business court for an evidentiary hearing on whether those directors are independent and disinterested. 

- Notice the timing, because you can do this before anyone has sued. The corporation gives notice to shareholders, the court holds a prompt evidentiary hearing, and under subsection (h), the court's determination is dispositive in later litigation.

- You can litigate your committee's independence in advance and carry that ruling into the transaction. SB 29 added the exact same machinery for derivative-demand committees.

== Litigation

=== Derivative standing.

- Next we look at standing and books and records, which together control the pipeline of litigation.

- Under Section 21.552(a)(3), a listed corporation, or an opt-in company with 500 or more shareholders, may impose a minimum ownership requirement for bringing a derivative suit. 

- That threshold is capped at three percent of the outstanding shares. Two features matter: three percent is a ceiling, not a mandate, and it can be set by bylaw, meaning the board can adopt it without a shareholder vote.

- Note the aggregation rule. SB 29 amended the definition of shareholder to include two or more shareholders acting in concert, so a coalition can stack holdings to clear the threshold.

- Unlike Delaware SB 21, there is no retroactivity here. This applies only to proceedings instituted on or after the effective date.

=== Example: Who could actually clear 3% of Tesla?

- Let us do the math on the three percent, because I want to know what it does in the world rather than on paper.

- Take Tesla. Three percent of its outstanding shares in August 2026 was about 118 million shares, worth roughly 43 billion dollars.

- Who holds that much? Elon Musk at 17.7 percent, and the big three index complexes: Vanguard, BlackRock, and State Street. 

  - [State Street crosses the line when its seven entities are rolled up; at the entity level it misses by hundredths of a point. The slide shows the entity figures.]

- Index funds are structurally the least likely derivative plaintiffs in the market. So the threshold does not literally bar suit; it hands the gatekeeping to holders who have never wanted it.

- For SpaceX, the answer differs in kind, with three percent being roughly 407 million shares. The holders who clear it are Musk plus the pre-IPO venture and strategic investors --- Alphabet, Valor Equity, and Founders Fund --- while the index complexes do not clear it at all.

- So the honest version of the argument is that the statute reassigns the gatekeeping function, and whether any coalition actually forms is an empirical question nobody has answered yet.

=== Books and records: an improper-purpose rule aimed at pre-suit discovery.

- The other half of the litigation pipeline is information. Section 21.218 keeps the standing requirement --- a six-month holder, or a five-percent holder. 

- It adds that corporate records do not include emails or texts unless they effectuate an action by the corporation.

- The bigger move is subsection (b-2). For listed corporations and opt-ins, a demand is per se not for a proper purpose if the corporation reasonably determines it is connected to a pending or expected derivative proceeding, or to an active or pending lawsuit where the parties are expected to be adversarial.

- That is a per se improper-purpose rule aimed directly at pre-suit discovery.

- Nevada removed the inspection section entirely for public companies, while Texas leaves it standing but declares the litigation-related demand improper. Either way, the plaintiff who needs documents before filing does not get them.

=== SB 29 § 15 "substantial benefit" settlements.

- The final piece of the litigation package is what winning is worth. 

- In Delaware, under Trulia, counsel could sometimes settle a case for supplemental disclosures and receive a fee award, provided the disclosures were plainly material. 

- SB 29 added Section 21.561(c). It says that a substantial benefit to the corporation does not include additional or amended disclosures made to the shareholders, regardless of materiality.

- That is a direct statutory kill of the disclosure-only settlement fee. Delaware declined to legislate on fees at all; Texas did it in one sentence.

== Courts

=== Texas has a statutory statewide court.

- So Texas has the code. Where do you litigate it?

- HB 19 created a statewide, trial-level Business Court with governor-appointed judges serving two-year terms, operational as of September 2024. Appeals go exclusively to the newly created Fifteenth Court of Appeals.

- HB 40, effective September 1, 2025, broadened qualified transactions and lowered the amount-in-controversy floor from 10 million to 5 million dollars.

- It also added jurisdiction over trade-secret disputes, permitted aggregation to meet the threshold, and authorized appointments for the remaining divisions. 

=== Jury trials can be waived.

- One feature of Texas courts historically was that business disputes went to a jury of non-experts. The Business Court supplies expert judges, and SB 29 lets a company drop the jury altogether.

- Under Section 2.116, the governing documents may waive the right to a jury trial for internal entity claims, and the waiver is enforceable even if not signed.

- Subsection (d) says you are treated as having agreed if you vote for the document, acquire shares while it is in place, or --- for a listed company --- simply continue to hold your shares after the board adds the waiver.

- Compare that to Nevada, which requires the waiver in the articles, meaning a stockholder vote. Texas permits it in the bylaws, which the board can adopt alone.

=== Which cases the Business Court can hear.

- Section 25A.004 sets the jurisdiction. It reaches actions involving governance, governing documents, or internal affairs, with a 5 million dollar floor.

- Subsection (c) is the key: the court has concurrent jurisdiction regardless of the amount in controversy if a party is a publicly traded company. For a public company, the monetary floor disappears entirely.

- The plaintiff's pleading determines the forum, as shown in Reed v. Rook TX, where the court initially kept the case under its broad governance jurisdiction. When Reed amended his complaint to delete the governance allegations and keep only a claim for money, the court found no jurisdiction and returned it to the district court.

=== Nevada and Texas, side by side.

- Put the two competitors side by side. 

- The liability floors are near-identical, but Texas requires pleading with particularity. 

- Officer exculpation is default in Nevada, but requires a charter election in Texas. 

- Nevada offers no inspection right for public companies; Texas makes the litigation-related demand per se improper.

- Nevada requires a jury waiver in the articles; Texas allows it in the bylaws. Nevada is still trying to build its constitutional business court; Texas has had one since 2024.

- If you are advising a controlled company, the real differences sit below the substantive standard --- who elects, what the plaintiff reads first, and who decides.

== Internal Affairs and the Forum

=== Background: The Internal Affairs Doctrine

- Let us turn to the boundaries of state power. The internal affairs doctrine says the law of the state of incorporation governs the relationships among the corporation, its directors, officers, and shareholders.

- One state's law governs them, wherever suit is brought. Delaware treats this as a mandatory rule of corporate law, commanded by the Constitution except in the rarest situations.

-  [Discussion map. Ask whether California's quasi-foreign statute, Section 2115, is a valid exception. The question is what counts as internal affairs, and what counts as procedural.]

=== The end of the "internal-affairs" doctrine?

- Texas pushed directly against this boundary. SB 29 added Section 1.056, which says managerial officials may consider the laws and decisions of other states, but a failure to do so does not imply a breach of Texas law.

- Section 21.562(a) is the provocation. It says that in a derivative proceeding brought in the right of a foreign corporation, Sections 21.555, 21.560, and 21.561 are procedural provisions that do not relate to internal affairs, and so apply notwithstanding the foreign jurisdiction's law.

- What are those sections? Section 21.555 is a 60-day stay for committee review, and Section 21.560 says no settlement without court approval. 

- Section 21.561 is the substantial benefit rule that kills disclosure-only settlement fees.

- Calling the first two litigation management is arguably fair. Section 21.561(c) sets what counts as a substantial benefit to the corporation, which is substantive, so the procedural label is perhaps misleading.

- Note the scope here: Section 21.419, the business judgment rule, is domestic-only. But Section 21.562(a) carries these three derivative-suit limits into Texas-forum litigation over a foreign corporation.

== Proxy Voting

=== Almost nobody attends the meeting. They send a representative instead.

- We move now from the boardroom to the proxy machinery. Public companies hold annual meetings to elect directors and vote on charter changes.

- Almost nobody attends. If you cannot attend, you appoint a proxy to vote on your behalf. 

- Because essentially every share is voted by proxy, the solicitation, not the meeting, is where the outcome is actually decided.

=== Federal law governs solicitation of proxies.

- Federal law, specifically Exchange Act Section 14(a) and Regulation 14A, governs that solicitation.

- Management files a proxy statement and sends a proxy card, which is the ballot. 

- Rule 14a-8, the town hall rule, lets a qualifying shareholder require the company to carry their proposal in the company's proxy statement, at the company's expense.

=== SB 1057 limits access to the proxy statement.

- Texas passed SB 1057 to limit that access. It applies to a nationally listed corporation that is either principally officed in Texas or listed on a Texas-based exchange.

- It is an opt-in: the corporation must amend its governing documents.

- Once elected, a proponent must clear three hurdles: hold shares worth at least 1 million dollars or 3 percent of voting shares; hold them continuously for six months through the meeting; and solicit holders of at least 67 percent of the voting power.

- Director nominations and procedural resolutions are carved out.

=== SB 1057 vs. Rule 14a-8, and the preemption question.

- Set that against the SEC's Rule 14a-8. The federal rule demands a longer hold --- one to three years --- but a far lower ownership threshold and no solicitation requirement.

- Texas permits aggregation among shareholders, while Rule 14a-8 prohibits it. So on that axis, Texas is the more permissive one.

- The preemption question is live. SEC Chair Paul Atkins noted in an October 2025 speech that proposals failing a state's properly established conditions should be excludable under Rule 14a-8(i)(1). 

  - [Note: A Chairman's speech is not Commission action.]

- Nobody took the invitation; Corp Fin received zero such requests for the season. The SEC is now proposing to rescind Rule 14a-8 altogether, allowing states to set their own rules.

== Proxy Advisors

=== Proxy advisors are firms that provide research, recommendations, and vote execution for shareholder meetings.

- Most shares are held by institutional investors who vote thousands of ballots a season. They purchase services from proxy advisors to manage this scale.

- ISS and Glass Lewis dominate this market. Their recommendations move votes at the margin.

=== Executives have long resented proxy advisors.

- Executives resent this influence. Jamie Dimon called them incompetent; Elon Musk called them corporate terrorists after ISS recommended against his pay package.

- The resentment focuses on executive pay and board oversight, and political attention followed, especially in Texas.

=== SB 2337 is Texas' attempt to rein in proxy advisors.

- Texas passed SB 2337, requiring a proxy advisor to give a specific financial analysis for any recommendation against management, and presuming advice resting on non-financial factors is not in shareholders' financial interest.

- ISS and Glass Lewis sued Attorney General Paxton. In August 2025, Judge Albright entered preliminary injunctions in both cases.

- The grounds the court gave were ERISA preemption, the First Amendment, and vagueness. The dormant Commerce Clause was pleaded by Glass Lewis, but the court did not rest on it.

- Why compelled speech? Because the Act makes an advisor tell clients its services are not being provided solely in the financial interest of shareholders, misleading the clients.

  - [Note: The trial was set for February 2026. The outcome is unverified, but the injunctions stand and the litigation continues. Do not state the case as terminated.]

=== The economics behind SB 2337 do not hold up.

- The economics here are straightforward. Shareholder voting is a collective-action problem, and proxy advisors lower the cost of informed participation.

- If the law makes it more costly to recommend against management than for them, the marginal recommendation shifts toward management regardless of the merits. 

- Investors also customize the advice they receive. Roughly 80 percent of funds take custom recommendations, not the benchmark advice. 

- Weakening these services would likely increase deference to management rather than enhance shareholder oversight.

  - [The citation for Other People's Votes is 115 Geo. L.J. (2026), without a pin cite.]

== The Exchange Listing Rules

=== Listing rules are a source of private governance regulation.

- The last layer of this is the listing venues. An exchange is a self-regulatory organization; its rulebook goes to the SEC for review.

- The rulebook contains quantitative standards, but also governance rules: board composition, committee requirements, and shareholder voting mandates. 

- Delaware even borrowed this definition recently, presuming a director independent if they meet the exchange's criteria.

=== Exchanges compete for listings, creating a race to the bottom for governance rules.

- Exchanges compete for listings. Remember WeWork in 2019, when Adam Neumann asked the NYSE and Nasdaq to ban meat and plastic in their cafeterias in exchange for the listing, and Nasdaq won.

- By filing to go public, WeWork's inner workings were exposed, and investors blanched.

=== Exchanges are already competing for Texas-based listings.

- We now have three exchanges competing for Texas-based listings. 

- TXSE was approved by the SEC in September 2025 as the 29th registered national securities exchange, and trading went live in July 2026. Corporate transfers are expected in Q4 2026, and primary listings --- IPOs --- in 2027.

- NYSE Texas launched in March 2025 in Dallas. Trump Media was its inaugural dual listing, followed by AT&T and others.

- Nasdaq Texas launched in March 2026. SpaceX dual-listed there at its IPO, giving the venue the largest market capitalization of the three.

=== Efforts to court new listings resemble WeWork.

- The competition resembles WeWork. NYSE Texas proposed offering complimentary services and visibility packages for dual listings.

- Their SEC filing stated plainly that they compete by improving the quality of services offered to listed companies.

=== TXSE's novel listing rule proposal.

- TXSE moved from being just a venue to being a governance actor.

- In August 2026, TXSE proposed mandatory mirror voting for uninstructed shares. This takes effect only upon SEC approval.

=== Mirror voting changes few outcomes and often opposes management.

- Rebuilt over half a million vote items, mirror voting flips only 0.4 percent of outcomes. 

- Over three-quarters of those flips go against management. It changes outcomes mostly on shareholder-sponsored governance items, like special meetings and independent chairs.

-  [Discussion map. The punchline is that mirror voting may not be as disruptive as feared, nor as management-friendly as TXSE hopes.]

- That covers the Texas package. We will pick up next week. 
